                    {
                        "translation_id": "03d5e8ce-78d9-4f27-97da-06262f536d57",
                        "source_entry_id": "bb339fde-731d-3b6c-9a61-fbbcdecce809",
                        "source_sense_id": "b961f056-1e6b-3bb6-b744-fa05703df4ec",
                        "source_sense_description": "en 2. bladeren van Cynara cardunculus",
                        "target_lang_word": "cardoon",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "cardoon",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "5acca979-314c-34ee-820e-b6977bab7ff9",
                                        "entry_id": "ca951f20-3a13-32ec-8759-81d4408ec18e",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Any of species Cynara cardunculus of prickly perennial plants which has leaf stalks eaten as a vegetable, related to the artichoke."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0e307357-eb3e-4d38-b1d7-717e0dbe173e",
                                                "sense_id": "5acca979-314c-34ee-820e-b6977bab7ff9",
                                                "text": "Means: m. Void, and emptie places between beds in gardens, reserved for speciall hearbes; such are the spaces left for Cardoons betweene rowes of Onyons."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "096edbe2-1e2c-471b-a7af-bfa0e97c759d",
                                                "sense_id": "5acca979-314c-34ee-820e-b6977bab7ff9",
                                                "text": "As I have already said, I nowhere saw the cardoon south of the Salado; but it is probable that in proportion as that country becomes inhabited, the cardoon will extend its limits."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e03f0d78-bbb1-4d00-bc2e-bcba67dded8f",
                                                "sense_id": "5acca979-314c-34ee-820e-b6977bab7ff9",
                                                "text": "In the sixteenth century, Ruellius speaks of the cardoon as a food that was appreciated as asparagus is today."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "7b0bf3b4-82a4-41e6-ac79-7c0da9bda637",
                        "source_entry_id": "bb339fde-731d-3b6c-9a61-fbbcdecce809",
                        "source_sense_id": "b961f056-1e6b-3bb6-b744-fa05703df4ec",
                        "source_sense_description": "en 2. bladeren van Cynara cardunculus",
                        "target_lang_word": "cardon",
                        "target_lang_code": "fr",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "cardon",
                                "target_lang_code": "fr",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "a5b0aaa9-3248-3c7d-9c79-b41469556661",
                                        "entry_id": "a81e64a0-b0c5-3598-bb27-5cbd279fdfc9",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Plante potagère (Cynara cardunculus L. subsp. cardunculus), de la même espèce que l’artichaut, et dont les feuilles sont comestibles. — Cynara cardunculus (wikispecies)"
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0ff405ae-d9ef-4a26-987f-160ca8ee2e0c",
                                                "sense_id": "a5b0aaa9-3248-3c7d-9c79-b41469556661",
                                                "text": "Le père avait commencé depuis plus d’une heure de buter des cardons."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c1872f30-6904-4d93-bbf1-eb0e3144ad91",
                                                "sense_id": "a5b0aaa9-3248-3c7d-9c79-b41469556661",
                                                "text": "En gratin, arrosé ou non de moëlle fondante, le cardon est au cœur des tables de fête."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "4ee549f7-d762-4f8d-af73-905b4873360e",
                                                "sense_id": "a5b0aaa9-3248-3c7d-9c79-b41469556661",
                                                "text": "Ses parents ne faisaient que la ratte, la carotte, la tomate, les salades et le cardon."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
